<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-subspace-top" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Subspace topology</title>
  We begin with the following proposition.
  <proposition xml:id="prop-subspace-top">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>(X,\tau)</m> a topological space and let <m>Y</m> be a subset of <m>X</m>. Following topologies defined on <m>Y</m> are all equivalent. 
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The topology <m>\tau_1=\{U\cap X:U\text{ an open subset of }X\}</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The <em>coarsest</em> topology <m>\tau_2</m> on <m>Y</m> for which the canonical inclusion <m>i\colon Y\hookrightarrow X</m> is continuous.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    (Universal property of subspace topology). The topology <m>\tau_3</m> on <m>Y</m> satisfying the following property.
                </p>
                <p>
                    For every topological space <m>(Z,\tau_Z)</m> and any function <m>f\colon Z\to Y</m>, <m>f</m> is continuous if and only if <m>i\circ f\colon Z\to X</m> is continuous. See the following diagram.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-universal-property-of-subspace-top">
                    <caption>The universal property of the subspace topology</caption>
                    <image width="40%">
                        <latex-image>
                            
\begin{tikzcd}
Z \arrow[d,"f",labels=left] \arrow[rd,"i\circ f"] \amp   \\
Y \arrow[r, hook,"i",labels=below]      \amp X
\end{tikzcd}
                        </latex-image>
                        
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </statement>
  </proposition>
  <proof>
    <p>
        The equivalence of topologies <m>\tau_1</m> and <m>\tau_2</m> is left as an exercise.
    </p>
    <p>
        We show that <m>\tau_3\subseteq\tau_1</m>. Consider the following diagram, where <m>1_Y\colon Y\to Y</m> is the identity map.
        <figure>
                    <caption></caption>
                    <image width="40%">
                        <latex-image>
                            
\begin{tikzcd}
(Y,\tau_1) \arrow[d,"1_Y",labels=left] \arrow[rd,"i\circ 1_Y"] \amp   \\
(Y,\tau_3) \arrow[r,"i",labels=below]      \amp X
\end{tikzcd}
                        </latex-image>
                        
                    </image>
                </figure>
                Note that <m>i=i\circ 1_Y</m>, and with the topology <m>\tau_1</m> the inclusion <m>i\colon (Y,\tau_1)\to X</m> is continuous. By the universal property of <m>\tau_3</m>, the identity <m>1_Y\colon(Y,\tau_1)\to (Y,\tau_3)</m> is also continuous. Hence, <m>\tau_3\subseteq\tau_1</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
        We now consider the following.
        <figure>
                    <caption></caption>
                    <image width="40%">
                        <latex-image>
                            
\begin{tikzcd}
(Y,\tau_3) \arrow[d,"1_Y",labels=left] \arrow[rd,"i\circ 1_Y"] \amp   \\
(Y,\tau_3) \arrow[r,"i",labels=below]      \amp X
\end{tikzcd}
                        </latex-image>
                        
                    </image>
        </figure>
        As <m>1_Y\colon (Y,\tau_3)\to (Y,\tau_3)</m> is continuous, using the universal property of <m>\tau_3</m>, we get that <m>i=i\circ 1_Y</m> is also continuous. Since the <m>\tau_1</m> is the coarsest topology that makes inclusion continuous, we have that <m>\tau_1\subseteq\tau_3</m>.
    </p>
  </proof>
  <definition xml:id="def-subspace-top">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>(X,\tau)</m> be a topological space, and let <m>Y</m> be a subset of <m>X</m>. The <em>subspace topology</em> on <m>Y</m> is any of the topologies defined by <xref ref="prop-subspace-top"/>.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
        Suppose that <m>Y_\alpha\subseteq X_\alpha</m>. We can consider a product topology on <m>\prod Y_\alpha</m> as well as subspace topology induced by a product topology on <m>\prod X_\alpha</m>. Are these topologies same on <m>\prod Y_\alpha</m>?
    </statement>
  </exercise>

      <example xml:id="GL-as-topological-group"><title>(Group of invertible matrices as a topological space)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          We consider <m>GL_n(\R)=\{A\in M_n(\R):\det A\neq 0\}</m> as a subset of <m>\R^{n^2}</m>, and we give subspace topology on <m>GL_n(\R)</m>. As the entries of product of matrices <m>A,B\in GL_n(\R)</m> depend continuously on entries of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, the multiplication map <m>\colon GL_n(\R)\times GL_n(\R)\to\R</m> is continuous. Furthermore, the entries of the inverse of <m>A</m> depends continuously on the entries of <m>A</m>. Hence, the inverse map <m>GL_n(\R)\to GL_n(\R)</m> given by <m>A\mapsto A^{-1}</m> is continuous.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

</section>